 think together in which condition we do  a retrain maybe below some threshold or  maybe in some condition where the data  change drastically so i really encourage  you to think about all of these things  and define them in advance once you  deliver the models  you might also consider to build an auto  retrain pipeline if this is a very  important model and the data changes a  lot  and i also encourage you to do a  retrospective after delivery to  understand what can we do better for the  next time  and  continue meeting about this project even  if the model is already in production  it's a very important model now right  it's working it's  answering some a question is solving  some use case we need to see it's still  on track even months later so continue  to meet and monitor  the model together and see that everyone  is happy with it  so the last part and don't worry it will  be very short just one slide  is about managing up to our leadership  we talked about managing down managing  across and now managing up  what does it even mean  so first of all managing up the first  thing about it is to understand how can  you help your manager succeed  because as a manager this should be  your primary goal you need to make sure  your manager is successful your manager  is happy and well appreciated because it  all comes down back to you if your  manager is successful then you are  successful your manager gets resources  and interesting projects you get these  things um and the very important to  define and share the vision and scope  with your team it will help your manager  represent you with the higher leadership  and give you everything that you need  and to align the work your team does to  the company goals so everyone would know  what does this ai really contribute to  our company  and  it's very helpful to communicate the  team plans and also communicate the  achievements we did in the past we used  the quarterly planning sessions  to to do that we invite all the  partners but also the leadership so they  would see our plans and they would also  see  all of our achievements  um and we as managers also need to find  opportunities to share the teamworks and  let the team members share their work on  their own  it's very important so everyone would  know all the great stuff that we are  doing  and something more that i do and many  people are very surprised by this i  collect feedback actively i have a  google doc in which i just copy paste or  write down when people give me or my  team feedback and i share this google  doc with my manager once a month so my  manager would know how with people in  what does people in my company  think about my team and about me and  how well are we doing this is very  helpful for the manager to see that  think about it  and and  all of this basically is an investment  in the future of your team  making your manager happy making your  managers think that you're on track and  that you're delivering good things for  the company will help you get more  resources get wrecks  and get new projects which are impactful  and interesting and aligned with the  vision of your team  so to summarize the data science  manager's role is basically to create  the platform for the team to succeed and  grow  and we do that with three things  managing down  encouraging growth innovation and  building a high performance culture  by managing across  creating trust and alignment with our  partners creating the right processes  for the project to succeed  and managing up  sharing and communicating with our  manager the vision and achievements  of the team and by that investing in the  future of the  team  so  thank you very much  and i will take questions now  thanks a lot shir  so  the most upvoted question uh is actually  a lot of people took pictures of your  slides would you mind sharing them  somewhere  uh sure yeah i can i can share the  slides somewhere and maybe pycon has  some kind of platform for that i'll have  a it has a question i guess you can  update it in the description so i think  you can might find it then in the  description just of this talk yeah and  and i guess there will be some kind of  video right shared in youtube  so you can also use it from there but  i'm i'm happy to share the slides also  you can you can email me if you want and  i can share the slides with you  so do you think technical knowledge is  inevitable for a good data science  manager oh that's a great question i  have so many discussions about this with  people and  i think it kind of depends on which  level of management you're talking about  if you're a first line manager and you  manage a team of data scientists  directly and not manager of data  scientists i think that technical  knowledge is crucial  but i with that i i think you don't have  to be  you know  the the most experienced data scientist  in your team to be a good data science  manager you need to have to be good  enough data scientists to understand the  work to understand the challenges  and of course you need to be a good  manager and invest in all the things we  talked about so it's a kind of  combination and as you go up the  leadership ladder so the details of  course  of the technical parts matter less yeah  great  so the next question is how do you make  sure research track time is not used as  buffer that is always used for origin  tasks  that's a great question and actually how  we implement the research track is not  always by five percent or or ten percent  of the ongoing task it is sometimes like  a quarter that someone takes to research  uh something after for example after a  big delivery someone would take a  quarter now to just research a new  direction so this is also you know more  effective way to deliver it um and  something else if you if you do use it  as a 10 percent or 50 track then you can  make sure you just block someday in your  calendar  to do some concentrated research work  so that's a tip i always give my people  but it's basically a  a question of discipline you know how  much you enforce yourself to to really  apply it yeah  yeah thanks so what do you think should  be the policy for firing people for  example netflix has the mentality that  good performance gives you a nice leaf  bonus  i didn't understand the question  to be honest i'm sure i can repeat it  okay yeah  so for example  sorry should the mentality for  holding someone in the team let's think  strictly on a performance base  be off  if you are  we keep going on the team only if you  are very very good performance so the  example from netflix  and then  low to medium performance should leave  the team and then you would  enforceably make them leave the team  or should you just keep low performing  the team until they leave  um well  i think that in general i didn't have  the pleasure yet to fire people  unfortunately um but i think that you  don't want low performance performers in  your team because they affect badly  other team members because if someone in  your team sees that someone else can you  know not do much and stay in your team  then it reflects on them they and and  also they might be dependent of this  person works so in general you don't  want low performance people in your team  and if someone gets to the point when  he's low performing then or he she's low  performing then it's the obligation of  the manager to communicate the  expectations to this person and reflect  to them you know their current situation  give them feedback and give them  consistent feedbacks a few times and  document the process and if after a  you know x times of consistent feedback  there is no improvement then yeah this  person should be fired because you know  companies should in the end should  produce some results you can't just pay  people for being low performance  um  i hope in the death answer your question  how much should that be  so sorry how much should  does define low performance for you  for how long and like for how much for  them six months  usually most of the companies that i  know of have something called pip that  is performance improvement plan which i  think is about  three months or four months that someone  has to be communicated then it's in the  situation and that's it get an  opportunity to to get improved  but even before you get into the pip  situation your manager should be  transparent enough to give you the  feedback and to help you improve and i  think that if a person really wants to  improve and really  suitable for a certain role then he or  she will approve but maybe this person  is just not suitable and could find  himself better in a another position or  another company so maybe it would be  better for for him or her to to to leave  if if they're not able to perform  thanks i think we have time for three  more questions maybe so what book  influenced your thinking the most on  this topic  that's a great question because as i  mentioned i really love books i read a  lot of them  i think the most basic one is the  effective manager so if you're starting  to be a manager i really recommend you  to read that one  and another one that was also you know  mind-blowing for me is multipliers which  talks about how we as manager and we are  in general as leaders of anything in  companies um need to  not always not be the smartest person in  the room but help others  be smarter so because together when we  are all working together we can reach  much further  and not just you know make yourself be  the best on account on the expense of  other people  thanks  what's the biggest mistake you made when  you started out as a manager  um  well  i i did many mistakes  [Laughter]  uh one uh one mistake we did was  actually to take on a project i was not  a manager then i was just starting  leading  being a data science lead but afterwards  a transition to a manager with this  project  and we started working on the project  with a company that would it wasn't  clear  it was an anomaly detection work in  security and it wasn't clear who would  actually use  the result of the anomaly detection  system and i think maybe some of you  also familiar with this kind of system  you know someone comes to you and say oh  we have a lot of data please do some  anomaly detection from it because maybe  there would be some security breach or  something like that um but no one really  has the tools or maybe the times to  actually consume  the alerts this system brings out so  my lesson from that is to make sure that  if you write some systems someone uses  the output it gets out  thanks  one last question so  i think it's easy and hard to answer is  there a tension between time spent  developing goals making plans and  reviews etc and time spent doing applied  data science work  uh  probably mean as a manager right i guess  so yeah  yeah well of course and i remember  myself in the first two years being a  manager always you know on with the  tension with the boundaries of how much  hands-on work should i do and how much  would i invest in management and i think  it really depends on where you work and  how big is your team because when you  work in a corporate and you have a lot  of  you know a bureaucracy and a lot of  management  stuff that you need to do then you have  less and less time for coding so as the  team grew i think in about seven people  i understood that i can't be much  hands-on and the best use of my time  would be to invest in more of long-term  planning in developing my team members  because that would be the most effective  investment for me as a manager and i  found myself doing hands-on work mainly  in hackathons which is i mentioned are  very very important for me  great thank you very much let's give her  one round of squads thank you thank you


